# Words and style

Keep words consistent by meaning, not by replacing every occurrence with a preferred synonym. A control label, an integration identifier and a person’s own device name serve different purposes.

Use [Home Assistant terminology](home-assistant-terminology.html) to find the native term, its household explanation, literal identifiers and easily confused meanings. Its [official glossary](ha-glossary.html), [entity and state language](entity-and-state-language.html) and [interface terminology](interface-terminology.html) record the reviewed vocabulary and version. A term list is not permission to replace native translations or a household's chosen names.

<h2 id="wg-020">WG-020 — Use a small, deliberate household vocabulary</h2>

**SHOULD.** Choose consistent authored terms for the same operation and different terms for different operations.

Use “Save” to retain an edit, “Delete” to remove a record, “Turn off” for the supported off operation, and “Cancel” to leave an unfinished step when nothing else is implied. “Remove from this dashboard” must not silently mean “Delete the device”. A scene is a saved set of device states; an automation’s configured triggers start a run, and its conditions determine whether its actions run. A condition becoming true does not by itself trigger the automation. Explain these distinctions where needed rather than relabelling native features.

